   "screen": "What computers do well, and what they cannot do",
   "title": "Figure 1.1.2 · Garbage In, Garbage Out (GIGO)",
   "alt": "Two rows pass through the same computer. Top row, input: the clerk types the meter reading 950; output: a bill for 950 units, correct. Bottom row, input: the clerk mistypes 95,000; output: a bill for 95,000 units, wrong but printed anyway. The computer in the middle follows the program exactly and cannot tell whether the data is sensible. Labels read Garbage In on the left and Garbage Out on the right: the output can only be as good as the input.",
   "caption": "The computer did nothing wrong in either row. A wrong result usually comes from bad data or human error, which is what GIGO means.",
