   "screen": "Embedded devices, game consoles and wearables",
   "title": "Figure 1.1.3 · An embedded system inside a microwave oven",
   "alt": "A dashed outline labelled Inside a microwave oven contains a block diagram. On the left are two inputs: the keypad for cooking time and power, and a door switch that senses whether the door is open or closed. Arrows lead to a chip in the centre labelled embedded microprocessor with a fixed program. Arrows lead from the chip to four outputs on the right: the display showing a countdown timer, heating switched on or off, the turntable motor that spins the plate, and a buzzer that beeps when done. A note says the user often does not know it is there. Three tags underneath read: one dedicated job, program fixed in memory, cheap, small, low power.",
   "caption": "An embedded computer has inputs, processing and outputs like any computer, but it is built into the machine it controls and does only that one job.",
