   "screen": "Ports, expansion slots and expansion cards",
   "title": "Figure 1.5.2 · Ports, expansion slots and an expansion card",
   "alt": "Top: the back panel of a desktop computer with seven ports drawn and labelled: USB-A, USB-C, HDMI, DisplayPort, VGA (the oldest video port), Ethernet (RJ45) and audio jacks. Bottom left: a motherboard with a CPU socket, four RAM slots and three expansion slots labelled PCIe x16 (long), PCIe x1 (short) and PCI (older). Bottom right: a graphics card, an expansion card with its own GPU chip, a metal bracket with ports and a row of gold edge-connector contacts along its bottom edge. A dashed arrow shows the card being pushed into a free slot. Sound, network and video cards all fit this way.",
   "caption": "A port is a socket on the outside for plugging in a device; an expansion slot is a connector inside, on the motherboard, for an expansion card that adds features.",
