   "title": "Figure 4.1.3 · Columns versus a table",
   "alt": "Left: a page with a full-width headline at the top, then a continuous section break, then text in two newspaper columns with a line between them. A dashed arrow shows the text flowing from the bottom of column 1 to the top of column 2. Right: a table of West End ferry times with a merged title row, a bold shaded header row (Ferry, Departs, Arrives) and three data rows: Morning 7:30 to 7:55, Midday 12:15 to 12:40, Evening 17:00 to 17:25, with the times right-aligned. Labels: a row goes across, a column goes down, a cell is one box. Never fake columns or tables with spaces or tabs.",
   "caption": "Use columns for flowing text, like a newsletter article. Use a table for data in rows and columns that must line up.",
