   "screen": "Mixed references: lock just the row or just the column",
   "title": "Figure 5.3.2 · Mixed references in a ticket ready-reckoner",
   "alt": "A ready-reckoner for Carnival tickets. Row 2 holds prices: General 15, Jump-up 25, VIP 40. Column A holds the number of tickets, 1 to 5. One formula, =$A3*B$2, was typed in B3 and filled right and down. For D7, arrows point left to A7 (5 tickets) and up to D2 (VIP price 40): $A7*D$2 = 5 times 40 = 200. $A3 has column A locked: copied across it stays in A, copied down it becomes A4, A5 and so on. B$2 has row 2 locked: copied down it stays in row 2, copied across it becomes C2, D2. A summary: B2 is relative, row and column both change; $B$2 is absolute, both fixed; B$2 is mixed, row fixed, column changes; $B2 is mixed, row changes, column fixed. Read the $ as lock the thing right after it.",
   "caption": "Every cell in the table looks left to column A for the number of tickets and up to row 2 for the price. That is exactly what the two $ signs lock.",
