   "screen": "Records, fields and sorting",
   "title": "Figure 5.4.2 · Why you must sort whole records",
   "alt": "Left: the Sports Day list as typed, with House, Student and Points; Zoe of Gold house has 10 points. Right: what happens if only the Points column is sorted, largest first, and the selection is not expanded. The points are reordered 10, 10, 10, 8, 8, 6, 6, 4 while the houses and names stay where they were, so Jaden now has 10 points, which was Zoe's score, and every record is split apart. Advice: always sort whole records, including every column and the heading row, and choose Expand the selection if Excel warns you. Each row is a record, each column is a field, and Points is the sort key.",
   "caption": "A record must stay together. Sorting one column on its own scrambles everyone's results.",
