   "screen": "Input masks: a template for typing",
   "title": "Figure 6.2.1 · How an input mask matches what is typed",
   "alt": "Two input masks drawn character by character. Phone, mask 000\\-0000: three 0 positions each take a required digit (5, 4, 2), the backslash makes the next character, a dash, appear exactly as written, and four more 0 positions take 1, 1, 8 and 7, giving 542-1187. Licence plate, a style, mask >LL\\-0000: the > converts the following letters to uppercase, so typing pm becomes PM in the two L positions, which each need a letter; then a literal dash and four digits, 1234, giving PM-1234. A key explains: 0 is a required digit; L is a required letter; > converts the next letters to uppercase; backslash shows the next character as typed; 9, ? and a are optional versions. A warning: a mask controls the pattern of typing, not the value; 000\\-0000 cannot stop someone typing 000-0000; a validation rule controls which values are acceptable.",
   "caption": "Each mask character stands for one position. The literal characters, like the dash, are filled in for the user.",
